<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-test-indep">
<title> Testing for independence </title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	a set of vectors <m> S </m> in a vector space <m> V </m> is provided.
	<ol>
		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-test-indep-use-proc">
			Use <xref ref="procedure-col-row-null-space-concepts-colspace-linear-indep-test" /> to test the vectors for independence. </li>
		<li>
			If the vectors are linearly dependent,
			express all dependence relations revealed in <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-test-indep-use-proc">part</xref>.
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> V = \R^5 </m> </p>

		<p><m> S = \{ (1, -4, 2, 6, -2), (0, -3, 3, 7, -7), (2, -5, 2, 3, -5) \} </m></p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Use the vectors as columns in a matrix and reduce.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 1 \amp  0 \amp  2 \\
				-4 \amp -3 \amp -5 \\
				 2 \amp  3 \amp  2 \\
				 6 \amp  7 \amp  3 \\
				-2 \amp -7 \amp -5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		The rank is <m> 3 </m>,
		which matches the number of vectors in <m> S </m>.
		Therefore, <m> S </m> is a linearly independent set.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> V = \matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R) </m> </p>

		<p><m>
			S = \left\{
				\begin{bmatrix}      2 \amp 1 \\  0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr} 7 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 3 \end{abmatrix},
				\begin{bmatrix}      5 \amp 3 \\  0 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix},
				\begin{bmatrix}      8 \amp 9 \\  2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
			\right\}
		</m></p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Use the coordinate vectors of the vectors relative to the standard basis of <m> \matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R) </m> as columns in a matrix and reduce.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				2 \amp  7 \amp 5 \amp 8 \\
				1 \amp  1 \amp 3 \amp 9 \\
				0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
				1 \amp  3 \amp 2 \amp 2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -5 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp  5 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		The rank is <m> 3 </m>,
		which is less than the number of vectors in <m> S </m>.
		Therefore, <m> S </m> is a linearly dependent set.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> V = \poly_4(\R) </m> </p>

		<p><md>\begin{split}
			S = \{
				x^4 + x^3 - 2 x^2 - x,
				x^4 - 2 x^2 + x + 3,
				x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + 1,
				\\
				5 x^4 + 4 x^3 - 6 x^2 + 4
			\}
		\end{split}</md></p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Use the coordinate vectors of the vectors relative to the <q>reverse</q> standard basis <m> \{ x^4, x^3, x^2, x, 1 \} </m> of <m> \poly_4(\R) </m> as columns in a matrix and reduce.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 1 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  5 \\
				 1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp  4 \\
				-2 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp -6 \\
				-1 \amp  1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp  3 \amp  1 \amp  4
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{bmatrix}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
			\end{bmatrix}
		</md>
		The rank is <m> 4 </m>,
		which matches the number of vectors in <m> S </m>.
		Therefore, <m> S </m> is a linearly independent set.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
